y recruited tens  of thousands of small channels with a  promise of better ad rates high quality  brand deals and the vague promise of  exposure and promotion with no solid  commitments in reality they delivered on  none of those and simply skimmed ab  greven e off the top of the 60,000  channels they had brought into their  network it finally came to a head when  cutie-pies racist practical jokes made  it clear that no one was at the wheel  that maker wasn't in the business of  content and talent development and were  in fact just more rent seekers luring in  creators with poorly backed promises of  getting paid and they stole money from a  lot of people so if you're a new video  platform and your core selling point  your big launch feature is a tip jar  forgive me if I'm having some deja vu  but I'm pretty sure I've heard this song  before I want to stress that point the  launch feature aspect we live in a world  after YouTube YouTube already exists  YouTube already has over a decade of  development missed development feature  creep and general design crust YouTube  has a number of vestigial features that  are good ideas but either they don't  work well or they're functionally  abandoned because they're just tacked on  or poorly implemented any new platform  try to compete doesn't really need to  reinvent the wheel they're not figuring  this out for the first time they just  need to figure out how to make certain  features work how to improve them how to  fold them into the core structure of  their service it's the launch features  of a platform that are going to tell you  the most about a platform's values both  in design and business what features  they consider to be core to the identity  and structure of the platform where are  their priorities there's a lot of  successful youtubers who would gladly  jump ship from YouTube to another  platform if that platform  offered features that on YouTube are  either lacking or just plain bad see  what youtubers actually 