ll  secondary data from names to  descriptions to captions and subtitles  and thumbnails be re-entered manually oh  and you lose existing comments views  votes and so on in the move and these  are just a few of these structural site  design issues with YouTube not even  addressing the cultural problems with a  lack of moderation unclear and unevenly  enforced community standards and easily  exploited automated systems there are a  wealth of deficiencies with YouTube that  could all be centered as a core selling  point for a competitor to bring this  back around I mentioned this but it's my  belief that you already employ people to  do research and development and did some  market research before you even started  building your platform to me that seems  like just a sensible business decision  you need to know your competitors you  need to know your users and you need to  know the market if you don't have that  well it doesn't exactly fill me with  confidence if you do have that and this  is the site that you chose to launch  these are the features that you  considered vital that you figured would  capture the audience that you're looking  for well it doesn't exactly fill me with  confidence  but simply if someone's roasting your  site on Twitter your co-founder  shouldn't be imploring them on Twitter  to submit feedback and make the site  better because you should already know  if you haven't done your research if you  haven't worked systems for security  moderation and collaboration into the  core infrastructure of the site if those  features aren't foundational to your  design then it's already too late it  means that you haven't built a youtube  competitor you've just built a youtube  clone and you're dooming yourself to the  same mistakes a Frankenstein product  built of cobbled together features that  don't properly integrate the charitable  reading is that you're just unprepared  which isn't a good look on its own but  the worst reading is that you're  Drifter